   "parameter_provenance": {
    "equation": "Equation 5, page 229: standard third-order Birch-Murnaghan finite-strain pressure equation with the isothermal K_T and K'_T at the chosen temperature.",
    "V0": "Tables 2 and 3, pages 228 and 231: the calculated one-atmosphere 300 K molar volume is 24.45 cm^3/mol. It is converted with the exact Avogadro constant to the conventional Pbnm bridgmanite cell with Z=4, giving 162.4007 A^3.",
    "K0": "Tables 2 and 3, pages 228 and 231: K_T=260.51 GPa at 300 K. The abstract rounds the same 300 K value to 261 GPa.",
    "K0_prime": "Abstract, page 226, and Equation 16, page 230: K'_300=4. The value is prescribed by the published temperature-dependent model and is fixed in this 300 K slice.",
    "volume_basis": "The article reports formula-molar MgSiO3 volumes in cm^3/mol. The existing orthorhombic Pbnm bridgmanite conventional cell has four MgSiO3 formula units, so both the reference and observation conversions use Z=4.",
    "temperature_ref": "The executable slice uses the source's explicitly tabulated 300 K reference. The source calls its ambient-volume table a one-atmosphere calculation and its elastic coefficients zero-pressure quantities; at the article's printed precision both define the conventional 300 K zero-pressure BM3 reference."
   },
   "notes": "Published 300 K isotherm of the two high-temperature Birch-Murnaghan models, which coincide at 300 K. The full Model 1 and Model 2 parameterizations are intentionally not represented: they require the article's quadratic temperature laws for thermal expansivity and compressibility together with its temperature-dependent K'_T convention, which the current Peritheos thermal reference-state model cannot encode exactly. The executable record is therefore restricted to 300 K and is not the default bridgmanite EOS. The natural-enstatite starting material contained less than 1% Al and Fe; the authors identify the transformed phase and reported volumes as MgSiO3 perovskite. No EOS-parameter errors, confidence convention, covariance, residual statistic, or fit weights are published.",
